// Test: a *hard* error from a std/ function is catchable, in every engine.
//
// The stdlib has two failure channels (REFERENCE.md §20b): environmental
// failures come back as a soft `Value::Error`, and programmer mistakes — a
// wrong argument type, an argument outside a function's domain — raise. The
// soft channel was well covered. The hard one was not, and in the register VM
// it did not work at all: `CallBuiltin` propagated the error with `?`, straight
// out of the interpreter loop, without ever looking for an armed `:!`.
//
// So `!? { m::ln(0.0) } :! ##_ { }` caught the error under the tree-walker and
// killed the program under `--vm`, for every function in every std/ module.
// Nothing noticed, because no corpus file had ever tried to catch one.

<# std/json => j
<# std/math => m

// Wrong argument type — a programmer mistake, not an environmental failure.
!? {
    >> j::decode(42) ¶
} :! ##_ {
    >> "json type error caught" ¶
}

// Outside a function's domain.
!? {
    >> m::ln(0.0) ¶
} :! ##_ {
    >> "math domain caught" ¶
}

!? {
    >> m::asin(2.0) ¶
} :! ##_ {
    >> "asin domain caught" ¶
}

// The soft channel is unaffected: malformed JSON returns an error *value*,
// which is tested with `$!` rather than caught.
// (no braces in the literal: `{}` is string interpolation in Zymbol)
bad = j::decode("[1, 2")
? bad$! {
    >> "soft parse error returned" ¶
}

// Execution continues normally afterwards.
>> j::encode([1, 2, 3]) ¶
>> m::ln(1.0) ¶
>> "end" ¶
